\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Preliminaries}
\date{2026-11-04}


\p{The notes assume some mathematical maturity: reading and writing proofs, and
the language of sets, functions and equivalence relations. They assume a course
in mathematical physics, at the level where the classical field equations are
met and where the potential, the field strength and gauge freedom are already
familiar. They also assume the measure-theoretic language of probability on
finite-dimensional real spaces, Borel sets, Lebesgue integration and monotone
convergence, which the [Gaussian laws](fdip-0005) use. Geometric calculus is
optional throughout; the one place it appears, \ref{fdip-00AA}, says so.}

\p{The algebra is that of a commutative ring with identity, its modules and its
algebras. A \vocab{ring} is a set with addition and multiplication: addition
makes it an abelian group, multiplication makes it a monoid, and multiplication
distributes over addition; every ring below is commutative and has an identity
#{1_R}. An #{R}-module is an abelian group #{M} with a scalar multiplication
#{R\times M\longrightarrow M} that is additive in each argument, respects
multiplication in #{R}, and sends #{1_R} to the identity map. An
#{R}-\vocab{algebra} is a ring #{A} with a \vocab{ring homomorphism}
#{\iota:R\longrightarrow A} whose image is central, so that #{A} is an
#{R}-module under #{r\cdot x=\iota(r)x}.}

\p{Those axiom lists are the algebra suite's \vocabk{ring}{ca-000U},
\vocabk{module}{ca-000X}, \vocabk{algebra}{ca-0016} and
\vocabk{ring homomorphism}{ca-0014}, and a tree that uses a further structure
links it where it uses it. The differential forms recalled in \ref{fdip-00AA} and
used in \ref{fdip-00AB} and \ref{fdip-00AD} are recalled rather than developed.}
